  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nBy Sunday, there is broad support for shifting the surface and \nelevated boundaries focusing the storms, much closer to our CWA. \nThis will begin a long duration of rain and thunderstorms near our \nlatitude. Initially, instability is questionable though Sunday,\nwith the main boundary still southwest of eastern Iowa. This \nseems to be reflected in 40-70 percent pops, by Sunday afternoon\nand evening, with relatively light QPF under 0.20\". \n\nOf particular note in this active period is the Monday shortwave.\nLatest guidance has this wave trending slower, resulting in the\ninitial warm front not reaching the area until Monday \nafternoon. This has lead to a decreasing trend in high \ntemperatures for Monday, though highs are still expected to be \nabove-normal due to the strong warm advection regime. This will \nalso establish instability across the warm sector supportive of \nshowers and thunderstorms. Guidance also suggests that vertical \nshear will be near 40-50 kts and thus allow for organized \nconvection. This is most likely with forcing along a descending \ncold front, though uncertainty with timing makes it unclear \nwhether this threat would be in the daytime or overnight hours.\n\nAs this frontal boundary passes through the area, it is expected to \nstall across the region. While there is some uncertainty in where \nexactly this boundary will set up, current guidance suggests it will \nbe along our south. This then provides the focus for rainfall over \nthe next several days, especially as southwesterly flow provides a \ncontinuous stream of moisture. Additional waves towards the latter \nhalf of the period will help push this boundary out of the region, \nthough chances for rain will remain. The near daily rainfall\npotential, offers a growing threat for heavy rainfall, and a\nrather widespread chance for 2-4 inches is growing in the\nregion. If this sets up over recent wet counties, a flood threat\ncould develop.",
